/surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><year>1988</year><article-title>Toxic Mexican folk remedies for the treatment of empacho: the case of azarcon, greta, and albayalde</article-title><source>J Ethnopharmacol</source><volume>21</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>31</fpage><lpage>39</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3199838</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b6-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bellinger</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name><name><surname>Leviton</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Rabinowitz</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Needleman</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Waterneaux</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><year>1986</year><article-title>Correlates of low-level exposure in urban children at 2 years of age</article-title><source>Pediatrics</source><volume>77</volume><fpage>826</fpage><lpage>833</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3714374</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b7-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bernard</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name><name><surname>McGeehin</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Prevalence of blood lead levels &#x02265;5 &#x003bc;g/dL among U.S. children 1 to 5 years of age and socioeconomic and demographic factors associated with blood lead level 5 to 10 &#x003bc;g/dL, Third National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey, 1988&#x02013;1994</article-title><source>Pediatrics</source><volume>112</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>1308</fpage><lpage>1313</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14654602</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b8-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brody</surname><given-names>DJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Pirkle</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name><name><surname>Kramer</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Flegal</surname><given-names>KM</given-names></name><name><surname>Matte</surname><given-names>TD</given-names></name><name><surname>Gunter</surname><given-names>EW</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1994</year><article-title>Blood lead levels in the US population. Phase 1 of the Third National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES III, 1988 to 1991)</article-title><source>JAMA</source><volume>272</volume><fpage>277</fpage><lpage>283</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8028140</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b9-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Gardner</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Sargent</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Swartz</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Hu</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Timperi</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001a</year><article-title>Effectiveness of housing policies to reduce children&#x02019;s lead exposure</article-title><source>Am J Public Health</source><volume>91</volume><fpage>621</fpage><lpage>624</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11291376</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b10-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Shenassa</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Tips</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001b</year><source>Small Area Analysis of Risk for Childhood Lead Poisoning</source><publisher-loc>Washington, DC</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Alliance to End Childhood Lead Poisoning</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="b11-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bryan</surname><given-names>SD</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Lead-contaminated drinking waters in the public schools of Philadelphia</article-title><source>J Toxicol Clin Toxicol</source><volume>42</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>287</fpage><lpage>294</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15362596</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b12-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><collab>CDC (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention)</collab><year>1984</year><article-title>Lead poisoning-associated death from Asian Indian folk remedies&#x02014;Florida</article-title><source>MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep</source><volume>33</volume><fpage>638</fpage><fpage>643</fpage><lpage>645</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6436668</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b13-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="book"><collab>CDC</collab><year>1991</year><source>Preventing Lead Poisoning in Young Children</source><publisher-loc>Atlanta GA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="b14-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><collab>CDC (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention)</collab><year>1994</year><article-title>Lead-contaminated drinking water in bulk-water storage tanks&#x02014;Arizona and California, 1993</article-title><source>MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep</source><volume>43</volume><fpage>751</fpage><fpage>757</fpage><lpage>758</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7935306</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b15-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><collab>CDC (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention)</collab><year>1997</year><article-title>Children with elevated blood lead levels attributed to home renovation and remodeling activities&#x02014;New York, 1993&#x02013;1994</article-title><source>MMWR Morb Mort Wkly Rep</source><volume>45</volume><fpage>1120</fpage><lpage>1123</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b16-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><collab>CDC (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention)</collab><year>2002a</year><article-title>Childhood lead poisoning associated with tamarind candy and folk remedies&#x02014;California, 1999&#x02013;2000</article-title><source>MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep</source><volume>51</volume><fpage>684</fpage><lpage>686</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b17-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="book"><collab>CDC</collab><year>2002b</year><source>Guidelines for the Management of Elevated Blood Lead Levels Among Young Children</source><publisher-loc>Atlanta, GA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="b18-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><collab>CDC (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention)</collab><year>2003</year><article-title>Surveillance for elevated blood lead levels among children&#x02014;United States, 1997&#x02013;2001</article-title><source>MMWR Surveill Summ</source><volume>52</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>21</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b19-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><collab>CDC (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention)</collab><year>2004a</year><article-title>Childhood lead poisoning from commercially manufactured French ceramic dinnerware&#x02014;New York City, 2003</article-title><source>MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep</source><volume>53</volume><fpage>584</fpage><lpage>586</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b20-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><collab>CDC (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention)</collab><year>2004b</year><article-title>Brief report: lead poisoning from ingestion of a toy necklace&#x02014;Oregon, 2004</article-title><source>MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep</source><volume>53</volume><fpage>509</fpage><lpage>511</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b21-ehp-116-1285"><citation citation-type="journal"><collab>CDC (Centers fo